.TH doubletake 1 "November 2026" "doubletake" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
doubletake \- AirPlay screen mirroring sender for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B doubletake
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B doubletake ctl
.RI [ flags ]
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B doubletake
is an AirPlay screen mirroring sender that streams your Linux desktop to Apple TV
and compatible AirPlay receivers. It supports both Wayland (PipeWire/xdg-desktop-portal)
and X11 screen capture with H.264 or capability-gated HEVC Main10 encoding.
.PP
The \fBdoubletake\fR binary dynamically links GStreamer and can adjust
video quality while streaming.
.PP
Default builds support ALAC audio and AAC-ELD through the external FDK-AAC
library. On Linux, the library is loaded when installed; otherwise ALAC is
used when offered. The selected codec must be advertised by the receiver.
.SH FEATURES
.IP \(bu 2
Full AirPlay mirroring protocol (RTSP/HTTP + encrypted video stream)
.IP \(bu 2
FairPlay SAP authentication
.IP \(bu 2
SRP-6a pairing with PIN and persistent credential storage
.IP \(bu 2
H.264 encoding with NVENC, VA-API, V4L2 stateful encoders, OpenH264, and x264
.IP \(bu 2
Capability-gated HEVC Main10/high-resolution encoding with NVENC or x265
.IP \(bu 2
ChaCha20-Poly1305 stream encryption
.IP \(bu 2
mDNS discovery of AirPlay displays and classic RAOP speakers
.IP \(bu 2
Daemon mode with multi-target streaming control via
.B doubletake ctl
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-target \fIADDRESS\fR
Connect to a specific AirPlay receiver by IP address or hostname.
With \fB\-no-video\fR, use mDNS to look up its advertised audio endpoint. An IPv6 link-local literal
requires an interface suffix such as fe80::1234%eth0.
.TP
.B \-device-id \fIID\fR
Expected receiver device ID. Use the saved pairing for this receiver and refuse
a device reporting a different ID when that pairing exists. Interactive
discovery supplies an expected ID automatically. Without one, an unknown
reported device ID is treated as a first connection.
.TP
.B \-port \fIPORT\fR
AirPlay port (default: 7000)
.TP
.B \-code \fICODE\fR
Pairing code shown on the receiver; prefer
.BR DOUBLETAKE_CODE .
.TP
.B \-port-range \fIMIN-MAX\fR
Confine local timing and audio UDP sockets to a range of at least three ports,
including streams started in daemon mode. Reserve three available ports per
simultaneous receiver. See
.B FIREWALL
below.
.TP
.B \-pair
Force new pairing even if credentials exist
.IP
Saved PIN/password pairings authenticate the receiver on reconnect. Older
credentials without a receiver key require pairing again once. A failed
identity check stops the connection; it never falls back to unauthenticated
pairing. After resetting or replacing a receiver, use this option and enter
the new on-screen code to establish its replacement identity.
.TP
.B \-cred-backend \fIBACKEND\fR
Credential storage backend: \fBfile\fR or \fBkeyring\fR (default: file)
.TP
.B \-creds \fIPATH\fR
Path to credentials file (default: ~/.config/doubletake/credentials.json)
.TP
.B \-fps \fIFPS\fR
Frames per second (default: 30)
.TP
.B \-resolution \fIMAX|WIDTHxHEIGHT[@REFRESH]\fR, \-s \fIMAX|WIDTHxHEIGHT[@REFRESH]\fR
Select the target encoded canvas. The default, \fBmax\fR, uses the highest
receiver-advertised mode which the selected local codec path can sustain.
Explicit dimensions are fitted within the receiver and codec ceilings while
preserving their requested aspect ratio, then normalized to even dimensions.
The optional refresh suffix selects the capture frame rate and overrides
\fB\-fps\fR; for example, \fB\-s 1920x1080@60\fR.
.TP
.B \-bitrate \fIKBPS\fR
Target bitrate in kbps. Zero selects an automatic resolution/FPS budget. The
default dynamically linked binary adjusts bitrate and frame rate from transport
telemetry during the session, using the weakest path when receivers share an
encoder. A
nonzero value disables runtime adjustment (default: 0)
.TP
.B \-target-latency-ms \fIMS\fR
Override both audio and video playout latency in milliseconds. Zero selects the
automatic AirPlay policy with separate screen and audio defaults (default: 0).
Daemon mode uses a 250 ms budget for mirror sessions. Direct audio playback
uses a 2-second audio lead. A nonzero value sets a common lead at startup,
subject to the receiver's supported latency.
.TP
.B \-source \fISOURCE\fR
Play video and audio from a local file or a direct HTTP(S) media URL.
Requires a GStreamer-enabled sender.
Use \fB\-source \-\fR with \fB\-target\fR to
read a streamable container from standard input. Pair first if an interactive
PIN is required. Playback stops at the end of input. Use \fB\-no-audio\fR
for video only.
For daemon mode, use \fBdoubletake ctl play\fR.
.TP
.B \-no-audio
Disable audio for video-only mirroring. Audio-only receivers require audio.
.TP
.B \-no-video
Send audio using a supported classic RAOP or realtime AirPlay 2 endpoint.
AirPlay 2 direct audio uses PTP or NTP according to receiver capabilities.
PTP audio sessions share one sender clock. In daemon mode this option applies
to all connections. Incompatible with \fB\-no-audio\fR.
.TP
.B \-audio-source \fISOURCE\fR
\fBmonitor\fR (default) copies the default local output and retains local
playback. Audio-only receivers follow the output level when available.
\fBoutput\fR creates a selectable doubletake output.
Select doubletake in sound settings to send applications or system audio to the
receivers. Its volume and mute controls apply to the captured audio. The daemon
shares this output across receivers and removes it when the last audio capture
stops. The selectable output requires pactl and GStreamer's pulsesrc with
PulseAudio or pipewire-pulse.
Default routing and existing application routes are not changed automatically.
Test mode generates a tone without creating an output.
.TP
.B \-hwaccel \fIMETHOD\fR
Encoder preference: \fBauto\fR, \fBnvenc\fR (NVIDIA), \fBvaapi\fR
(Intel/AMD), \fBv4l2\fR (stateful V4L2 encoders, common on ARM SoCs),
\fBopenh264\fR (software), or \fBnone\fR (x264, or x265 for
forced HEVC).
Only \fBauto\fR tries fallback encoders. An explicit method fails if its
required GStreamer encoder is unavailable. The default is \fBauto\fR.
.TP
.B \-video-codec \fICODEC\fR
Screen codec: highest usable \fBauto\fR (default), forced \fBh264\fR, or
forced \fBhevc\fR. Automatic mode selects a receiver-advertised high-resolution
HEVC canvas only after the local NVENC 4K timing preflight succeeds; otherwise
it retains the ordinary H.264 canvas. Forced HEVC may also use x265.
Main10 output does not fabricate HDR metadata for an SDR capture source.
.TP
.B \-test
Use synthetic video instead of screen capture. Unless
.B \-no-audio
is also set, generate an audio test tone.
.TP
.B \-x11-window-id \fIID\fR
Capture one X11 window by decimal or hexadecimal window ID. This option is used
only with X11 capture.
.TP
.B \-x11-window-name \fINAME\fR
Capture one X11 window by name. Window IDs are unambiguous, so prefer
.B \-x11-window-id
when available.
.TP
.B \-no-cursor
Omit the pointer from screen capture.
.TP
.B \-debug
Enable verbose protocol logging
.TP
.B \-daemonize
Run in daemon mode for multi-target control via
.B doubletake-ctl
.TP
.B \-socket \fIPATH\fR
Daemon control socket path. The default is
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/doubletake.sock ,
or
.I /tmp/doubletake.sock
when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.B \-help, \-h
Show command-line help and exit.
.TP
.B \-version
Show the version and source commit, then exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Discover and connect to an AirPlay receiver on the network:
.B doubletake
.TP
Connect to a specific AirPlay receiver:
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77
.TP
Pair with a PIN (saves credentials for reuse):
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-pair \-code 1234
.TP
Connect to a receiver with "Require Password" enabled:
.B DOUBLETAKE_CODE=... doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77
.TP
Stream with a custom frame rate and bitrate:
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-fps 30 \-bitrate 4500
.TP
Force lower bitrate on weak Wi-Fi:
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-bitrate 3000
.TP
Use hardware acceleration (NVIDIA):
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-hwaccel nvenc
.TP
Use OpenH264 software encoding:
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-hwaccel openh264
.TP
Run in daemon mode:
.B doubletake \-daemonize
.TP
Debug mode with verbose logging:
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-debug
.SH DAEMON MODE
When run with \fB\-daemonize\fR, doubletake runs as a background service that can control
multiple simultaneous connections. Use
.B doubletake ctl
to manage the daemon.
.PP
Daemon status distinguishes an on-screen PIN from a configured password so UI
clients can present the appropriate single prompt. Both values remain
submittable with the compatible
.B doubletake ctl pin
command when exactly one receiver is waiting. For concurrent prompts, use
.B doubletake ctl connect \fITARGET\fR \fIPIN-OR-PASSWORD\fR
to submit a credential to a specific receiver.
.SH FIREWALL
For each stream, doubletake binds three consecutive local UDP ports: timing at
\fIN\fR, audio control and receiver retransmission requests at \fIN+1\fR, and
outgoing audio data at \fIN+2\fR. Receiver-initiated NTP probes the timing port.
Screen mirroring with PTP and sender-initiated NTP use outbound timing.
Direct AirPlay 2 PTP audio also requires inbound UDP ports 319 and 320 from
the receiver. If doubletake cannot bind them, it tries AirPlay 2 with NTP.
If the receiver rejects NTP setup, doubletake can try its advertised classic
RAOP endpoint. On Linux, binding the PTP ports may require CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE.
The receiver can send audio retransmission requests to the control port.
.PP
The event and video data channels are outbound TCP connections to ports returned
by the receiver. doubletake does not require an inbound TCP firewall rule.
Router port forwarding and Internet exposure are not required.
.PP
The operating system selects ephemeral UDP ports unless
.B \-port-range
is set. On a default-deny host firewall, select a range and allow inbound UDP
to that range from each receiver or from the trusted LAN subnet. For example:
.PP
.B doubletake \-target 192.168.1.77 \-port-range 60000-60010
.PP
.B sudo ufw allow from 192.168.1.77 proto udp to any port 60000:60010
.PP
Daemon mode shares the configured range among its streams. Reserve at least
three available ports for every simultaneous receiver.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.IP \(bu 2
Go 1.25 or later, a C compiler, pkg-config, and GStreamer/FDK-AAC development headers (source builds)
.IP \(bu 2
GStreamer 1.16 or later (with plugins-base, plugins-good, plugins-bad, plugins-ugly, libav)
.IP \(bu 2
libfdk-aac.so.2 or libfdk-aac.so.1 to enable AAC-ELD audio
.IP \(bu 2
pactl and PulseAudio or pipewire-pulse for the selectable audio output
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBopenh264\fR method requires the GStreamer \fBopenh264enc\fR element
.IP \(bu 2
PipeWire (Wayland) or X11 for screen capture
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/doubletake/credentials.json
Default location for saved AirPlay credentials
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
Bash completion for options, encoder and codec choices, and file paths is
included in release packages and source installations. Install the
.B bash-completion
package and start a new shell to load it automatically.
